WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Welcome. This class gives you a reliable
mental model for

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Model Context Protocol. You will separate
host, client, and server

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
responsibilities, trace lifecycle
negotiation, distinguish prompts,

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
resources, and tools, design narrow tool
contracts, and keep application

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
policy portable when providers expose
different MCP integration surfaces.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.500
Start with three roles. The host is the AI

7
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:43.000
application. It owns the user experience,
consent, policy, model access,

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:49.500
aggregation, and decisions about what
crosses boundaries. The host creates

9
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:56.000
one MCP client for each server connection.
A client maintains

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.500
session and negotiated-capability state,
routes protocol messages, and isolates

11
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:09.000
that connection from others. A server
exposes a focused set

12
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.500
of prompts, resources, or tools. It may be
a local

13
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:22.000
process or a remote service. Local does
not automatically mean

14
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.500
safe, and remote does not automatically
mean untrusted. Assess identity,

15
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:35.000
code or service provenance, credentials,
data flow, and actions. One

16
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.500
server must not observe another server's
data merely because the

17
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:48.000
host can access both.

18
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.273
Consider a learning assistant connected to
a file server and

19
00:01:53.273 --> 00:01:58.545
a ticket server. The file client
negotiates resource and read-tool

20
00:01:58.545 --> 00:02:03.818
capabilities. The ticket client negotiates
search and comment tools. A

21
00:02:03.818 --> 00:02:09.091
ticket result must not flow to the file
server unless

22
00:02:09.091 --> 00:02:14.364
the host makes an explicit, authorized
decision. Each server receives

23
00:02:14.364 --> 00:02:19.636
only the context required for its
operation. If the ticket

24
00:02:19.636 --> 00:02:24.909
server fails, the file session can remain
healthy. If one

25
00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:30.182
server changes tools, only its capability
state changes. This separation

26
00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:35.455
makes data movement, failure,
authorization, and incident response

27
00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:40.727
inspectable instead of creating one
invisible pool of tools and

28
00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:46.000
context.

29
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Negotiate before operating. During
initialization, client and server exchange

30
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
protocol revision and capabilities. Only
after initialization completes may normal

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
operations use the features both sides
support. Treat negotiated state

32
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
as runtime evidence. A configured tool
name does not prove

33
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
the connected server currently exposes
tools, supports notifications, or

34
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
implements the expected revision. Record
server identity, requested and agreed

35
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:35.000
protocol, advertised client and server
capabilities, and fallback behavior. If

36
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:42.000
a required capability is absent, fail
closed or degrade through

37
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:49.000
a declared path. Do not invent support,
call an unnegotiated

38
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:56.000
operation, or silently substitute a more
powerful capability.

39
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Prompts, resources, and tools have
different control models. Prompts are

40
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.000
commonly user-selected templates.
Resources are application-selected

41
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.000
context. Tools are functions the model may
propose calling. These

42
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.000
labels help design the interface, but none
creates authorization. A

43
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.000
resource can contain stale facts,
malicious instructions, or sensitive data.

44
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
A tool result can attempt to redirect the
model. A

45
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.000
discovered tool can still be denied by
host policy. The

46
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
host decides which servers and primitives
are exposed, what context

47
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
is sent, what requires user confirmation,
and which trusted executor

48
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.000
may act. Protocol compatibility enables
exchange. It does not certify

49
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
the server, validate content, grant
permission, or prove a side

50
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.000
effect.

51
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Make a tool narrow and inspectable. Give
it a stable

52
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:22.000
name and a decision-oriented description
that distinguishes it from similar

53
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:29.000
tools. Constrain inputs with required
fields, explicit types, enums, formats,

54
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:36.000
bounds, and rejected additional
properties. Where supported, publish an

55
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:43.000
output schema and return structured
success and error variants. Separately

56
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:50.000
document side effects, data sent, identity
and object authorization, approval

57
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.000
rule, idempotency, limits, postcondition,
retryability, and recovery. A schema can

58
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:04.000
prove that workspace ID has the expected
form. It cannot

59
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:11.000
prove the workspace belongs to the caller.
An output schema

60
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:18.000
can make a result parseable. It cannot
prove the draft

61
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.000
was created in the real system.

62
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.231
Keep the product contract above provider
adapters. Define the required

63
00:06:30.231 --> 00:06:35.462
capability, approved server identity,
allowed primitive and tool set, data

64
00:06:35.462 --> 00:06:40.692
policy, approval rule, stable result,
telemetry, and recovery once. Then

65
00:06:40.692 --> 00:06:45.923
map provider SDK objects, request fields,
remote connector identifiers, supported

66
00:06:45.923 --> 00:06:51.154
transports, approval surfaces, beta
status, limitations, and errors in

67
00:06:51.154 --> 00:06:56.385
replaceable adapters. Different providers
and frameworks may expose MCP in

68
00:06:56.385 --> 00:07:01.615
different products or maturity stages. Do
not claim identical behavior

69
00:07:01.615 --> 00:07:06.846
from a shared acronym. Verify each adapter
against current first-party

70
00:07:06.846 --> 00:07:12.077
documentation and the same application
tests. Unsupported behavior must be

71
00:07:12.077 --> 00:07:17.308
explicit, not emulated silently when it
changes safety or user

72
00:07:17.308 --> 00:07:22.538
control. Record the exact adapter version,
verified feature subset, test

73
00:07:22.538 --> 00:07:27.769
date, and rollback route so later changes
can be reproduced

74
00:07:27.769 --> 00:07:33.000
and compared.

75
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:40.000
Sketch one host with two MCP servers. For
each connection,

76
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:47.000
name the client, server identity, required
capability, data sent, tool

77
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:54.000
exposed, approval point, postcondition,
and fallback when negotiation fails.

78
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:31.000
Checkpoint. Configuration lists a write
tool, but the connected server

79
00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:38.000
did not advertise tool capability during
initialization. Should the client

80
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:45.000
call that tool because configuration says
it exists?

81
00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:50.429
Do not call it. Configuration describes
intent, while initialization records

82
00:10:50.429 --> 00:10:55.857
what this session supports. Use the
declared no-tool fallback or

83
00:10:55.857 --> 00:11:01.286
stop with missing-capability evidence. If
you chose to try the

84
00:11:01.286 --> 00:11:06.714
call, move capability validation before
operation. If you assumed the

85
00:11:06.714 --> 00:11:12.143
server was unsafe, refine the conclusion:
absence may be version,

86
00:11:12.143 --> 00:11:17.571
configuration, or server behavior, but the
safe operational result is

87
00:11:17.571 --> 00:11:23.000
still no unnegotiated call.

88
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:29.400
Open the MCP contract activity. Draw the
host and two

89
00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:35.800
isolated connections, record
initialization and fallback behavior,

90
00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:42.200
define one read and one write tool, and
map the

91
00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:48.600
portable contract into one provider
adapter. Retain the architecture,

92
00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:55.000
lifecycle record, contracts, and adapter
assumptions.

93
00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.400
When ready, begin the knowledge check. You
will identify host

94
00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:09.800
policy ownership, enforce negotiated
capabilities, separate schema from

95
00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:14.200
authorization, preserve one client per
server, and choose a portable

96
00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:18.600
adapter boundary. The assessment begins
only when you choose Begin

97
00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:23.000
knowledge check.

98
00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:27.333
Keep roles separate, negotiate before use,
treat primitives as untrusted

99
00:15:27.333 --> 00:15:31.667
inputs or proposals, own policy in the
host, and verify

100
00:15:31.667 --> 00:15:36.000
every adapter.
